0,Purisimeño language - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Purisimeño_language,"March 6, 2025 - Purisimeño was one of the Chumashan languages traditionally spoken along the coastal areas of Southern California near Lompoc.",Purisimeño,Chumashan
1,Chumashan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Chumashan_languages,"February 25, 2026 - There is very little documentation of Purisimeño. Ineseño, Barbareño and Ventureño each had several dialects, although documentation usually focused on just one. Island Chumash had different dialects on Santa Cruz Island, San Miguel Island and Santa Rosa Island, but all speakers were relocated to the mainland in the early 19th century. John Peabody Harrington conducted fieldwork on all the above Chumashan ...",Purisimeño,Chumashan
2,Category:Chumashan languages - Wikipedia,https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Category:Chumashan_languages,Wikimedia Commons has media related to Chumashan languages.,Purisimeño,Chumashan
3,About: Purisimeño language,https://dbpedia.org/page/Purisimeño_language,"Purisimeño was one of the Chumashan languages traditionally spoken along the coastal areas of Southern California near Lompoc. It was also spoken at the La Purisima Mission. A vocabulary of ""La Purrissima or Kagimuswas (Purismeno Chumash)"" was collected by Henry Wetherbee Henshaw in 1884.",Purisimeño,Chumashan
4,Purisimeño - California Language Archive,https://cla.berkeley.edu/languages/purisimeno.html,"Map of the Chumashan languages (Robert F. Heizer, ed. 1978. California. (Handbook of North American Indians, Volume 8.) Washington: Smithsonian Institute.) Purisimeño is a member of the Chumashan language family; the others are Barbareño, Samala (Ineseño), Interior Chumash, Cruzeño, Obispeño, and Ventureño.",Purisimeño,Chumashan
5,Chumashan languages — Grokipedia,https://grokipedia.com/page/Chumashan_languages,"January 14, 2026 - The Chumashan languages form a ... Chumash (Cruzeño), and Southern Chumash (including Ventureño, Barbareño, Ineseño, and sometimes Purisimeño).[3] All Chumashan languages became extinct in the twentieth century following the death of their last fluent speakers, though ...",Purisimeño,Chumashan
6,"Chumashan languages - Alchetron, The Free Social Encyclopedia",https://alchetron.com/Chumashan-languages,"October 11, 2024 - There is very little documentation of Purisimeño. Ineseño, Barbareño and Ventureño each had several dialects, although documentation usually focused on just one. Island Chumash had different dialects on Santa Cruz Island and Santa Rosa Island, but all speakers were relocated to the mainland in the early 19th century. John Peabody Harrington conducted fieldwork on all the above Chumashan ...",Purisimeño,Chumashan
7,Chumashan languages,https://en-academic.com/dic.nsf/enwiki/942092,"Obispeño was the most divergent Chumashan language. Ineseño and Barbareño may have been dialects of the same language. There is very little documentation of Purisimeño. There were several different subdialects of Ventureño. Island Chumash had different subdialects spoken on Santa Cruz Island and Santa Rosa Island, but all its speakers were relocated to the mainland in the early 19th century.",Purisimeño,Chumashan
